Why the AGPL, and not a permissive licence
------------------------------------------
OpenLearn runs on a server. With a permissive licence, a company could take the
code, improve it, host it as a paid service and never publish a line back: the
community would keep maintaining the free version while a competitor sells a
better one built from it. Section 13 of the AGPL closes that gap — whoever offers
the software over a network must offer the corresponding source to its users,
modifications included.

Hosting it commercially is still allowed. Keeping those improvements secret is
not. That is the balance this project wants: anyone may build a business around
OpenLearn, nobody may do it by closing what the community wrote.


